            ],
            "context": "example",
            "id": null,
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "admonition",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "It’s important to note that specifying a "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "throws"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " clause in the test method has no effect on the outcome of the test. JUnit Jupiter does not interpret a "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "throws"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " clause as an expectation or assertion about what exceptions the test method should throw. A test fails only if an exception is thrown unexpectedly or if an assertion fails."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null,
            "type": "note"
          }
        ],
        "context": "section",
        "id": "uncaught",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Uncaught Exceptions"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Assertions in JUnit Jupiter are implemented using exceptions. The framework provides a set of assertion methods in the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " class, which throw "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "AssertionError"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " when an assertion fails. This mechanism is a core aspect of how JUnit handles assertion failures as exceptions. See the "
              },
              {
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "writing-tests/assertions.html"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "xref",
                "target": "writing-tests/assertions.html"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " section for further information about JUnit Jupiter’s assertion support."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "admonition",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Third-party assertion libraries may choose to throw an "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "AssertionError"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " to signal a failed assertion; however, they may also choose to throw different types of exceptions to signal failures. See also: "
              },
              {
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Third-party Assertion Libraries"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "xref",
                "target": "writing-tests/assertions.html#third-party"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null,
            "type": "note"
          },
          {
            "context": "admonition",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "JUnit Jupiter itself does not differentiate between failed assertions ("
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "AssertionError"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ") and other types of exceptions. All uncaught exceptions lead to a test failure. However, Integrated Development Environments (IDEs) and other tools may distinguish between these two types of failures by checking whether the thrown exception is an instance of "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "AssertionError"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null,
            "type": "tip"
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "In the following example, the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "failsDueToUncaughtAssertionError()"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " method throws an "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "AssertionError"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ". Since the exception is not caught within the test method, JUnit Jupiter will mark the test as failed."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "blocks": [
              {
                "context": "dlist",
                "id": null,
                "items": [
                  {
                    "description": {
                      "blocks": [
                        {
                          "blocks": [
                            {
                              "context": "listing",
                              "id": null,
                              "inlines": [
                                {
                                  "kind": "text",
                                  "value": "private final Calculator calculator = new Calculator();\n\n@Test\nvoid failsDueToUncaughtAssertionError() {\n\t// The following incorrect assertion will cause a test failure.\n\t// The expected value should be 2 instead of 99.\n\tassertEquals(99, calculator.add(1, 1));\n}"
                                }
                              ],
                              "language": "java",
                              "roles": [],
                              "title": null
                            }
                          ],
                          "context": "open",
